# s4_onnx_and_spill (re-derived order, 2026-08-04)
#
# basis      s3_trace_nograd peak 2482984 kB (steps.csv now 2,506,360, 0.93% apart):
#   heap:python3.10+0x13cc27      867.0 MB  0.341   the ONNX export buffer (io.BytesIO doubling)
#   heap:libstdc++.so+0xf4979     867.0 MB  0.341   the same object's serializer-side twin
#   heap:libc10.so+0x4a675        433.0 MB  0.170   the torch tensors (parameter set + Beaver)
#   anon:[heap]                   130.0 MB  0.051
# two humps  conversion ~2.48 GB (ONNX buffer) and Beaver ~2.52 GB (parameter set), within their spread.
#            Each mechanism alone refused by the build:
#   off-path/x_onnx_at_beaver_peak   ONNX alone:  -2.16% against a 9.76% spread, and that spread
#                                    IS the two humps (min 2,279,084 = conversion hump lowered,
#                                    max 2,516,624 = Beaver hump untouched)
#   off-path/x_spill_at_convert_peak spill alone: +380 kB, spread 0.386%, i.e. the conversion
#                                    hump standing exactly where it stood
# cost       ONNX free (-3.7% wall), spill paid (+1.4% vs 0.441%): first paid step; free phase exhausted
#            (s2/s3 skips, the two runs above).
#
# object_skipped: heap:libstdc++.so+0xf4979 (the ONNX buffer's serializer twin, 867.0 MB)
#   C recorder key artefact; one bytes object in the census; the single-file export removes both.
#
# impl_snapshot: bert_instrumented_cpu.py levers/plaintext_free_cpu.py levers/triple_share_free_cpu.py levers/trace_nograd_cpu.py levers/onnx_single_file_export_cpu.py levers/encrypt_spill_cpu.py
# Snapshot: driver plus imported lever modules, as named in shaft-cpu/impl/stepenv/s4_onnx_and_spill.env.
# pool_attacked: host
# object_attacked: bytes:- held_by=frame
# (re-measured 2026-09-29, freeze_mode=maxparty: in s3's heavier-party frozen table the ONNX buffer is
# heap:bytes:- held_by=frame, 433.5 MB, largest named row; same buffer as held_by=tuple below.)
#
# python census re-measurement 2026-08-11 (jobs 46065393-98): one bytes object, 413.4 MB, held by a
# tuple, largest at s3's peak. Removed vs s3's table:
#   -893.3 MB  anon:residual                      the allocator giving pages back after both fixes
#   -413.4 MB  bytes:- held_by=tuple              the ONNX export buffer, i.e. This
#   -413.0 MB  torch:torch.float32 held_by=dict   the spilled parameter set
OMP_NUM_THREADS=8
SHAFT_LEVERS=plaintext_free_cpu,triple_share_free_cpu,trace_nograd_cpu,onnx_single_file_export_cpu,encrypt_spill_cpu
